{{also|Abbot}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abbot}}, {{m|enm|abbod}}, {{m|enm|abbed}}, from {{inh|en|ang|abbat}}, {{m|ang|abbad}}, {{m|ang|abbod}}, from {{der|en|la|abbās||father}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀββᾶς}}, from {{der|en|arc|אבא||father|tr=’abbā|sc=Hebr}}. {{doublet|en|abba|abbé|bwana}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.ət/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.ət/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abbot.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abbot.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{homophones|en|Abbot}}
* {{rhymes|en|æbət|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The superior or head of an [[abbey]] or [[monastery]]. {{defdate|First attested around the early 12<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref>{{R:CDOE|page=2}}</ref><ref>{{R:SOED5|page=3}}</ref>
#: {{ux|en|The newly appointed '''abbot''' decided to take a tour of the abbey with the cardinal's emissary.}}
# The [[pastor]] or [[administrator]] of an [[order]], including minor and major orders starting with the minor order of porter.
# A [[layman]] who received the abbey's revenues, after the closing of the monasteries.
# {{lb|en|archaic|British slang}} A brothel-owner's [[husband]] or [[lover]].<ref name=barrere>{{R:Barrere|volume=1|page=3}}</ref><ref name=farmer>{{R:Farmer|volume=1|pages=4}}</ref>
# {{lb|en|archaic|British slang}} A [[ponce]]; a man employed by a [[prostitute]] to find [[client]]s, and who may also act as a [[bodyguard]] or equivalent to a [[bouncer]].<ref name=barrere /><ref name=farmer />

